; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The world: its name, and how far apart its storeys stand -- the one part of it
; the engine reads. Every map this world lays, the first floor and each one after
; it, stands its storeys that far apart. Everything else here is the game's own.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
World
  Name = Dungeon
  ; How far apart two storeys are, in world units. Ten, because that is how tall
  ; the kit's own wall comes out at a ten-unit cell — KayKit models a wall
  ; exactly one module high — so the wall holding up a raised room
  ; is one wall piece rather than one and a fraction. A cell is also ten across,
  ; which makes a one-cell stair climb at forty-five degrees.
  LevelHeight = 10
  ; How many cells a side a floor that mixes biomes is WALKED at for each one it
  ; is drawn at. Two: ten-unit cells walked as five-unit ones, so what stands in
  ; the way is its own shape rather than the whole of its cell -- a grove's trees
  ; are trunks with room between them, and a hero goes through where a boss goes
  ; round. Every rule counted in cells is still counted in ten-unit ones.
  NavigationCellsPerCell = 2
End

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The camera.
;
; The keys still pan and the wheel still zooms; this adds the oldest control in
; the genre on top of them, and the two work at once. Resting the cursor against
; the edge of the screen pushes the view that way, so moving it costs nothing --
; the mouse is already where the player is looking.
;
; The speed is a share of what the keys move at rather than a figure of its own,
; and the keys' speed is itself a function of how far out the camera is. So a
; shove moves the same amount of SCREEN at every zoom, instead of being right
; at one height and wrong at the others.
;
; An EdgeMargin of 0 turns it off, which is what every other game on this client
; gets unless it asks.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Camera
  ; How close to the edge, in window pixels. Wide enough to hit without aiming,
  ; narrow enough not to fire while crossing the screen.
  EdgeMargin = 14
  EdgeSpeedPercent = 100
End

Run
  ; How long the death message stays up before the next dungeon appears.
  RespawnDelayFrames = 60

  ; How long the finished floor stays open after the boss falls, in logic frames
  ; (30 = one second). Not decoration: the boss leaves something behind, and a
  ; floor that closed in the same frame would take it away before anyone could
  ; walk to it. It also gives the fight a moment to have been won in.
  DescendDelayFrames = 75

  ; And how long the last one stays up, which is longer on purpose: a death is an
  ; interruption and a win is an ending, and an ending wants looking at.
  VictoryFrames = 150

  ; What the banner says at either. The rest of the words the player reads are in
  ; Hud below; these two are here because they belong to the run rather
  ; than to the panel.
  ; Which hero walks into the dungeon, by his creature template in data/units/.
  ; The same word heads his Hero block and his four DungeonSkill blocks, so
  ; changing this line swaps every one of them together.
  ;
  ;   Hero    the archer — weak, fast, outranges the floor, loses the moment
  ;           anything reaches him
  ;   Knight  the swordsman — slow, armoured, has to walk in
  ;
  ; A line rather than a screen because a choosing screen is a separate piece of
  ; work; this is what makes the second one playable enough to be balanced.
  DefaultHero = Rogue

  DiedWord = You died
  WonWord = You won
  ; And between floors. %d is the floor he is going down to. A word rather than a
  ; string built in Java, which is what it was -- the only thing the player read
  ; that was written in English in a source file.
  NextDepthWord = Chuqurlik %d

  ; What stands where every floor lets the heroes in, with them round it: the
  ; fountain, which mends whoever stands near it -- see data/props/fountain.duke.
  ; A floor with no room for it at the way in goes without. Empty for nothing.
  WayIn = Fountain
End

Moment
  Name = LevelUp
  Effect = GoldenPillar
  Scale = 1
End

Moment
  Name = BossDown
  ; The level's own light, half as big again: the floor is his.
  Effect = GoldenPillar
  Scale = 1.5
End

Moment
  Name = Arrived
  Effect = Arrival
  Scale = 1
End

Combat
  ; Left over from when there was one kind of monster. Every kind now carries its
  ; own SenseRadius and ChaseRadius below, and nothing reads these — kept only
  ; because a file that silently ignores a setting is worse than one that says so.
  SkeletonSenseRadius = 36
  SkeletonChaseRadius = 72
  SkeletonRepathFrames = 10

  ; How close a HERO walks before he stops and lets his weapon work, for one who
  ; does not say. Each monster has its own CloseDistance further down; each hero
  ; may now have his own too, in his Hero block — and had better, because
  ; the number below is an archer's.
  ;
  ; ★ IT HAS TO BE INSIDE HIS OWN REACH, and the game clamps it to four fifths of
  ; that whatever is written here. One figure for every hero was fine while there
  ; was one hero: a swordsman reaching 11 stopped at 48 and stood four body
  ; lengths from a skeleton swinging at nothing, until it walked the rest of the
  ; way and hit him. From a chair that is a hero who will not fight until he is
  ; provoked, which is not what the number looks like it does.
  ;
  ; He carries a bow that reaches 60, so he stops well short and shoots. Kept
  ; inside that reach rather than equal to it: stopping at your own maximum means
  ; parking on the edge of it, where a step in any direction — his own, or the
  ; thing walking at him — puts the target outside and he stands there doing
  ; nothing. The margin is what makes the range usable rather than theoretical.
  ;
  ; This is the number that decides how the game is played. Lower it towards 4
  ; and he walks into everything and brawls, which is what he used to do.
  CloseDistance = 48
  HeroRepathFrames = 10

  ; How far in front of itself a walking thing looks for a body, in world units.
  ;
  ; The answer to a monster shuffling in a doorway. With a body in that space it
  ; STANDS STILL until the space clears; without one it walks. The locomotor's
  ; own answer is to step aside, and stepping aside works in the open and turns
  ; into a twitch in a corridor -- it finds a clear diagonal, takes it, is pushed
  ; back, and tries again thirty times a second.
  ;
  ; A little more than one step, so the answer comes before the collision rather
  ; than after it. Much larger and things stop a body's width short of each other
  ; and a queue forms behind nothing; much smaller and they touch first and the
  ; shuffle is back.
  WayAheadProbe = 5

  ; How a monster that keeps its distance backs away: straight away from him if the
  ; stone allows, and otherwise turned this many degrees further aside each try,
  ; left before right, this many tries each side. Past that it is cornered and
  ; fights where it stands.
  RetreatTurnDegrees = 30
  RetreatTurns = 3

  ; Where what a monster calls up rises: its skill's Radius away, first toward where
  ; it was aimed, and then turned this many degrees further aside each try, one way
  ; before the other, this many tries each way -- only on open floor it can see, never
  ; in stone, on another storey or on somebody. At the Skeleton Summoner's Radius of
  ; 16 a step of 45 puts two spots twelve units apart, a skeleton and a half.
  SummonTurnDegrees = 45
  SummonTurns = 4

  ; The hero shoots arrows that are really there: they leave the bow, cross the
  ; distance, and the damage lands when they arrive. At 170 a shot is in the air
  ; about a third of a second over his full range — long enough to see, and long
  ; enough that a monster can die from an arrow loosed before it moved.
  ;
  ; They chase rather than lead, so a shot never misses; what the flight costs is
  ; time. Ten times a monster's pace, or they would not catch anything.
  ArrowTemplate = Arrow
  ArrowSpeed = 170
  ; How far in front of him it appears. Started at his own position it comes out
  ; of his chest; the bow is held out ahead, and ahead is where he is facing —
  ; he turns to shoot, so his heading is the line of the shot.
  ArrowMuzzleOffset = 5

  ; What a stunned creature wears while it stands dazed -- see the Effect below,
  ; and StunFrames on the fire mage's fireball. Blank, and a stun is not drawn.
  StunLook = Stunned
End

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Stars over a stunned head, for as long as the stun: whatever stuns plays it on
; whoever it stunned (see ArrowUpdate), and Combat's StunLook above names it.
;
; No layer says how long it lasts. The client is told the longest StunFrames any
; skill has (Main.measureLooks), so the number is written once, on the skill, and
; the picture follows it -- as the frost on whoever a nova caught follows its
; SlowFrames.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Effect
  Name = Stunned
  ; Stunned again while it burns, the picture lasts to the new stun's end rather
  ; than being dropped, as the stun itself starts its count again.

  Layers = [
    ; A ring of light turning over his head, as a dazed man's world turns: above a
    ; hero's twelve units and clear of the mage's hat, and fading over the
    ; last sixth of its run, so the player sees the stun about to lift.
    Layer
      Name = Halo
      Type = AURA
      Texture = kit/effects/particles/twirl_02.png
      Blend = Additive
      Count = 1
      Size = [8, 8]
      Colour = [0xFFF4B0, 0xFFD24A]
      Alpha = [0.6, 0.6]
      FadeIn = 0.05
      FadeOut = 0.15
      Spin = 240
      Height = 15
      TurnJitter = 0
    End,

    ; And the stars on it: a few at a time, each gone in half a second, so the
    ; ring is always being drawn again round wherever he stands.
    Layer
      Name = Stars
      Type = AURA
      Texture = kit/effects/particles/star_04.png
      Blend = Additive
      Count = 6
      Rate = 10
      Radius = 4
      Life = [0.4, 0.6]
      Size = [2.4, 1.2]
      Colour = [0xFFFBE0, 0xFFC830]
      Speed = [0, 0]
      Direction = NONE
      Spin = 180
      Height = 15
    End
  ]
End

Progression
  ; The hero starts at level 1 and everything here is what levels 2 and up add.
  ; Level 1 grants nothing, so his template reads as the starting hero rather
  ; than as the hero minus his first level.
  ; ★ FIFTEEN, and the number is not free. A level is a POINT now -- see
  ; SkillRanks -- and three ordinary skills at four ranks each plus an ultimate
  ; at three is exactly fifteen. So a hero who reaches the top has everything,
  ; and every level before that is a decision about the ORDER rather than about
  ; what he ends up with. Move this and the two stop agreeing: at fourteen he
  ; never finishes, at sixteen the last level has nowhere to go.
  MaxLevel = 15
  ; How far the ordinary three may drift apart. Two: enough to favour one without
  ; abandoning the others, and what stops a skill being taken four times running
  ; while the other two sit at nothing.
  SkillSpread = 2

  ; Experience to reach level 2, and how much more each level after that costs.
  ; A skeleton is worth ExperienceValue in its template.
  XpBase = 30
  XpStep = 15

  ; ★ WHAT A LEVEL IS WORTH IS NOT WRITTEN HERE ANY MORE. His health, speed, mana
  ; and the weight of his blow all come out of his attributes, which grow by what
  ; his own Attribute lines say -- see Attributes below and his Hero
  ; block. HealthPerLevel, DamagePercentPerLevel, ManaPerLevel
  ; and ManaRegenPerLevel went rather than staying beside them: two sources for one
  ; figure are two dials a balance pass has to keep in step.

  ; Percent of incoming damage removed per level, and the floor it may not pass.
  ; Armour is the one thing a level still gives directly: it belongs to no
  ; attribute. Armour that reaches zero is not a tough hero, it is a game with no
  ; way to lose.
  ArmourPercentPerLevel = 5
  MinDamageTakenPercent = 40

  ; How long "Level 2!" stays on screen, in logic frames (30 = one second).
  LevelUpBannerFrames = 60
  ; ★ MANA FOR A KILL, and 0 is the shipped answer rather than the timid one.
  ; Paying for kills makes mana a reward for fighting, which pulls against what
  ; it is here for: with it off, the only way to get mana back is to wait, and
  ; the waiting IS the decision. Turn it up and the mage can fight his way back
  ; to a meteor, which is a different and easier game.
  ;
  ; There is no kill event on this side of the engine -- the experience module
  ; is rts's -- so what this actually watches is his experience going up. In
  ; this game nothing else raises it.
  ManaPerKill = 0

  ; What a point of his primary attribute adds to his blow -- see the attributes below.
  DamagePerPrimary = 1.0
End

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The attributes, and what a point of each is worth to every hero alike.
;
; A hero is one number per Attribute block below, and his Hero block
; says what he starts with, what each level adds, and which one is his PRIMARY.
; Everything else is worked out from them, in one step:
;
;   health = MaxHealth in his template  + each attribute x its HealthPerPoint
;   speed  = Speed in his template      + each attribute x its SpeedPerPoint
;   mana   = MaxMana in his block       + each attribute x its ManaPerPoint
;   attack = Damage in his template     + his PRIMARY    x DamagePerPrimary (in Progression)
;
; So a strength hero who finds strength gets both halves -- more health and a
; harder blow -- and an archer who finds strength gets only the health.
;
; ★ THE LIST IS THIS FILE'S. The blocks' order is the order a hero's attributes are
; held in and the order the panel draws them. Another attribute is a block here, an
; Attribute line in each hero who has any of it, and a picture in icons/stats --
; and no Java. ShortName is how a hero's block names it, Word what the panel calls it,
; Icon its picture; a figure a block leaves out, it adds none of.
;
; ★ DECIMALS ARE READ EXACTLY, never through a float: at most two places on a figure
; here and one on a hero's attribute, and 0.155 is refused rather than rounded.
; Attributes are held in tenths and these in hundredths, so every figure is one sum
; of integer products -- the same on every machine however many levels he has taken.
;
; What comes back on its own is not here on purpose. It is each hero's own
; HealthRegen and ManaRegen, fixed, and no attribute moves it.
;
; Smaller than a Dota's 18 health and 12 mana a point, because these heroes are
; smaller: their pools are fifty to a hundred and twenty, and twelve mana a point
; of intelligence would treble the mage's by his fifteenth level.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; His body: every point is health.
Attribute
  Name = Strength
  ShortName = STR
  Word = Kuch
  Icon = icons/stats/stat_strength.png
  HealthPerPoint = 12
End

; His legs: every point is speed.
Attribute
  Name = Agility
  ShortName = AGI
  Word = Epchillik
  Icon = icons/stats/stat_agility.png
  SpeedPerPoint = 0.15
End

; His pool: every point is mana.
Attribute
  Name = Intelligence
  ShortName = INT
  Word = Aql
  Icon = icons/stats/stat_intelligence.png
  ManaPerPoint = 5
End

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; What is left on the floor, and what he carries.
;
; A monster that dies may leave something behind: a chest, lying where it fell.
; Walking over it does nothing. A click on it sends him for it -- the right
; button, as on anything else he is sent at -- and it is his when he gets there,
; if his bag has room. A thing in his bag counts for as long as it is in it: the
; right button takes one in hand, and the left puts it down on the floor where
; it is pointed, where it lies for anyone to pick up again.
;
; ★ THREE ALIKE ARE ONE OF THE NEXT LEVEL. Three of the same thing at the same
; level in his bag join into one, worth the three together, up to TopLevel. The
; second level also brings the thing's Extra, and every level after is three of
; the one before, Extra and all:
;
;   level 1   +3 of its attribute
;   level 2   +9, and its Extra's value      (three of level 1)
;   level 3   +27, and three times the Extra (three of level 2)
;
; A third alike needs no room of its own: it joins as it comes in.
;
; The kinds a thing may be are what a hero is made of: ATTACK (percent of his
; blow), HEALTH (flat maximum health), ARMOUR (percent taken off what reaches
; him), MANA (flat maximum mana) and ATTRIBUTE (whole points of the attribute its
; Attribute line names -- see Attribute). What the dungeon leaves today is
; attributes alone. An Extra is HEALTH_REGEN or MANA_REGEN (whole points a
; second, on top of what comes back on its own) or ATTACK_SPEED (percent quicker
; between blows).
;
; Which monster leaves what is drawn from the run's own seed and the monster's
; own id -- so the same seed drops the same things off the same monsters, whatever
; order the player kills them in.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
LootDrops
  ; The creature a dropped item becomes. Naming none turns loot off entirely.
  Template = Chest
  ; How often an ordinary monster leaves something. Loot that always drops is not
  ; loot, it is a tax on killing things.
  DropPercent = 15
  ; The thing guarding the way down always leaves something. It is the one fight
  ; on the floor the player cannot walk past.
  BossDropPercent = 100
  ; How close he has to walk before a thing changes hands, picked up or put down.
  ; A cell is 10 units, so this is "standing on it" with room to spare.
  PickupRange = 14
  ; Nothing is worth more for being found deeper: what joins has to be alike, and
  ; a deeper floor's gauntlet worth four would never make three with two worth three.
  ValuePercentPerDepth = 0
  ; How long the panel says what he found, in logic frames (30 = one second).
  NoteFrames = 90
  ; How many things his bag holds. A full bag leaves the next one lying -- unless
  ; it is the third of a set, which joins as it comes in.
  Slots = 6
  JoinCount = 3
  TopLevel = 3
  FullWord = Sumka to'la
  ; Under a thing the pointer is on, and beside the pointer while one is in hand.
  TakeHint = O'ng tugma: olish
  DropHint = Chap tugma: yerga qo'yish
  ; What each Extra is called when the pointer rests on a thing that has one.
  HealthRegenWord = Jon/s
  ManaRegenWord = Mana/s
  AttackSpeedWord = Hujum tezligi
End

; Icon is the picture his bag draws it with, a path from the resource root.

; His body: strength, and from the second level health coming back quicker.
LootItem
  Name = Gauntlet
  DisplayName = Kuch qo'lqopi
  Icon = icons/stats/stat_strength.png
  Kind = ATTRIBUTE
  Attribute = Strength
  Value = 3
  Weight = 10
  Extra = HEALTH_REGEN
  ExtraValue = 2
End

; His legs: agility, and from the second level quicker blows. In percent: 2 would
; take less than one frame off a blow that comes every 24 to 34 of them, which is
; nothing a hand can feel, so the second level is a fifth quicker and the third
; three fifths.
LootItem
  Name = Boots
  DisplayName = Chaqqon etik
  Icon = icons/stats/stat_agility.png
  Kind = ATTRIBUTE
  Attribute = Agility
  Value = 3
  Weight = 10
  Extra = ATTACK_SPEED
  ExtraValue = 20
End

; His pool: intelligence, and from the second level mana coming back quicker.
LootItem
  Name = Tome
  DisplayName = Donolik kitobi
  Icon = icons/stats/stat_intelligence.png
  Kind = ATTRIBUTE
  Attribute = Intelligence
  Value = 3
  Weight = 10
  Extra = MANA_REGEN
  ExtraValue = 2
End
